  "agentMessages": [
    {
      "line": 3,
      "text": "I’ll build this as a small static data visualization so it can ship with GitHub Pages, using only local bundled data and a lightweight test suite. I’m going to inspect the workspace and tooling first, then create the app, docs, tests, metadata, and publish it."
    },
    {
      "line": 10,
      "text": "The repo is empty apart from the run artifacts, and the local Node/GitHub CLI toolchain is ready. I’m going to turn the actual `codex-events.jsonl` from this run into a static “run pulse” visualization, which keeps the data real and avoids external services."
    },
    {
      "line": 17,
      "text": "I’ve got the shape now: a dependency-free static site plus a Node parser that converts JSONL events into browser data. I’m going to add the source, tests, docs, and Pages-ready `public/` files in one scoped pass."
    },
    {
      "line": 20,
      "text": "The core project files are in place: parser, generator, static UI, tests, README, and docs. Next I’m creating the lockfile, generating the data artifact from the real run log, and running the test suite."
    },
    {
      "line": 27,
      "text": "Tests are passing against both synthetic edge cases and the bundled run log. I’m checking the GitHub account/repo target now so the metadata can be written with truthful URLs before the first commit."
    },
    {
      "line": 34,
      "text": "The GitHub owner is `jamiegonzalez929`, so I can now fill the project metadata and Pages URL deterministically. I’m adding a GitHub Pages workflow that deploys the generated `public/` directory rather than moving documentation around to fit branch-based Pages constraints."
    },
    {
      "line": 37,
      "text": "Metadata and deployment wiring are added. I’m going to regenerate the data once more, rerun tests, and do a quick local HTTP check so the static app is verified through the same serving path people will use."
    },
    {
      "line": 46,
      "text": "Port `4173` is already occupied on this machine, so I’m replacing the fixed Python server script with a small Node static server that automatically tries the next port. That keeps the documented run command accurate even when the default port is busy."
    },
    {
      "line": 59,
      "text": "The local HTTP smoke test passed and I stopped the server. I noticed the README still mentioned the old Python server, so I’m tightening that documentation before the final data generation and commit."
    }
  ],
